00:00:59.607 --> 00:01:01.190
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
this is super cool.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:02.450
I really like this one.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.030
Not only does this mean
that you have lower latency

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.950
for all of your
users, but it means

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.479
that even if a region happens
to go down, you're still safe.

00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:10.520
MELANIE WARRICK: Exactly.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.290
MARK MANDEL: So
that's pretty sweet.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:13.310
I love Cloud Spanner.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:14.570
It's sort of really
cool in that you're

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.692
like, oh, I would like
something to happen when

00:01:16.692 --> 00:01:17.900
a user does a thing in Japan.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.420
But I need it to be consistent
when somebody in Switzerland

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:21.392
needs to see that.

00:01:21.392 --> 00:01:22.850
And you can, which
is pretty sweet.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:23.725
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:01:23.725 --> 00:01:27.320
So the other thing of the week
is that Kaggle actually ran

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.780
a survey where they
wanted to get information

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.910
about the people who are
using Kaggle and the data

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:33.160
scientists that are out there.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.580
And they got 16,000 responses,
which is pretty impressive.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.800
And they've already done
some analysis of their own.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.494
It's got the data set that's
out there for others to explore.

00:01:40.494 --> 00:01:42.410
Some of the interesting
points that they found

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:45.020
were things like the average
age of data scientists, which

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.420
is apparently hovering
around early 30s.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.400
And just looking
at actually what

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.190
people are making in
different locations,

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.230
the US versus other countries.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:58.112
And of course the US is a higher
salary for that specific role.

00:01:58.112 --> 00:02:00.070
But yeah, I'd highly
recommend checking it out.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:03.500
We have links for
the Kaggle data set

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.770
as well as a podcast that was
separately done on our blog.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:07.520
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:08.936
Actually, so I
didn't realize this

00:02:08.936 --> 00:02:11.180
before, they did the
survey but they also

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.300
released the data set.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.960
So if you wanted to play with
the Kaggle data set on Kaggle,

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.229
then you can Kaggle it?

00:02:16.229 --> 00:02:17.270
MELANIE WARRICK: Exactly.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.200
MARK MANDEL: All right, cool.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:18.699
Awesome.

00:02:18.699 --> 00:02:19.797
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:19.797 --> 00:02:21.380
MELANIE WARRICK: Not
confusing at all.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:22.629
MARK MANDEL: No, that's great.

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:23.600
All right, fantastic.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.810
Finally, we'd be remiss if
we didn't mention the change

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.640
in name for very good reasons.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.330
So you may be familiar with
Google Container Engine.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:33.350
We talk about it a lot.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:36.230
It is now Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:39.140
Reason being that it has now
been released that there is now

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:42.290
certified Kubernetes
program so that you can then

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.150
go, oh, this provider here?

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:45.890
It's a good Kubernetes
installation.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:46.970
So they're providing
all the things

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:48.303
that the certification provides.

00:02:48.303 --> 00:02:50.280
And once you are
certified, you are

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.280
able to use the name
Kubernetes in your product.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.770
Since we are
certified, yay, we're

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:58.120
able to now call ourselves
the Google Kubernetes

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.120
Engine, which I'm going
to mess up all the time.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.020
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.770
We're both probably
going to mess that up.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:05.420
But GKE now is
officially something

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.041
that you can easily attribute
to in terms of the name.

00:03:08.041 --> 00:03:10.290
All right, so let's go ahead
and get to the interview.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.510
MARK MANDEL: Let's do that.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.830
Today is a very exciting day.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:18.560
We are joined by one of our
most remarkable teammates, Colt

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:19.190
McAnlis.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:19.310
COLT MCANLIS: Wow.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:20.340
MARK MANDEL: How are
you doing today, Colt?

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.548
COLT MCANLIS: I'm doing good.

00:03:21.548 --> 00:03:22.490
That's a lot of setup.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:23.019
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:23.019 --> 00:03:25.310
I'm really afraid of what's
coming next if that's the--

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:26.750
MARK MANDEL: No pressure,
no pressure at all.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:28.070
COLT MCANLIS: Most
remarkable, gosh.

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:28.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
guess the first question

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:30.890
is, why are you so remarkable?

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:31.400
Who are you?

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.699
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:33.699 --> 00:03:34.990
COLT MCANLIS: Nobody, actually.

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:35.735
I'm a toaster repairman.

00:03:35.735 --> 00:03:37.220
Or at least that's what
I like to tell people.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:37.929
It's funny story.

00:03:37.929 --> 00:03:39.428
So I'll get to who
I am in a second.

00:03:39.428 --> 00:03:41.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: [LAUGHS]
This starts well.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:41.600
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.247
There was a legitimate
period of time

00:03:43.247 --> 00:03:45.080
when no one really
understood what developer

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:46.400
advocates at Google did.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:47.600
And so I would go to
conferences and they'd

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:48.410
be like, what do you do?

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:49.993
And I'm like, I'm a
toaster repairman.

00:03:49.993 --> 00:03:51.670
And they bought it.

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:52.580
They're like, legit?

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:53.240
And I'm like, yeah, yeah.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:54.680
It's my job to make sure
all the toasters are

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:55.700
networked properly.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.370
And they're like, whoa.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:03:59.350
It's amazing.

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:01.819
Seeing the people's eyes
like, that's your job?

00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:03.360
That's amazing, how
do I get in that?

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:04.020
So.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:04.300
Anyhow, sorry.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.020
MARK MANDEL: That's
Google for you.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:05.356
Yeah.

00:04:05.356 --> 00:04:06.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:07.390
COLT MCANLIS: Google "shoe"
and people think really

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:08.510
that we make shoes.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:09.830
No, my name is Colt McAnlis.

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.920
I'm a developer advocate here
working on performance and data

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:16.010
compression, focusing dominantly
on performance for the Cloud

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.950
right now, how you can write
faster applications, cheaper

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.560
Cloud applications, take
your existing architecture,

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.990
tune it, make it better, make
it faster, make it cheaper.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:25.025
All that fun stuff.

00:04:25.025 --> 00:04:25.941
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:04:25.941 --> 00:04:28.370
So I'm going to do it in
the Simpson's way of like,

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:30.610
you might remember me
from previous series

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:31.800
as Compressor Head?

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.050
Tell us a little bit about it.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:34.341
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:34.341 --> 00:04:36.650
So there's a couple of
ways we can play that game.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:39.500
Yeah, so you may remember me
from a series called Compressor

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.870
Head where the point of it--

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.200
I'm a huge cooking show fan.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.140
I don't know if you've ever
seen Good Eats with Alton Brown.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.690
I find his
cinematography is great.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:49.250
I mean, the fact
that people can sit

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:51.770
down and watch a cooking show
and become enamored with it.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.464
We tried to figure out how we
can duplicate that at Google

00:04:54.464 --> 00:04:55.380
with different things.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:58.370
And I thought to myself, what
is the driest subject matter we

00:04:58.370 --> 00:04:59.360
have?

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:00.530
Data compression.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:03.320
And I was like, how do we make
data compression and Alton

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:04.370
Brown work together?

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:07.000
And so we tried to break down
these extremely difficult,

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.610
statistically defined
algorithms that basically drive

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:12.490
the back end of everything
we do on the internet

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:13.924
and make it physical.

00:05:13.924 --> 00:05:15.340
So how do we show
data compression

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:19.450
using sticky notes and
pet dinosaurs and markers

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:20.625
and sliding doors?

00:05:20.625 --> 00:05:22.750
And that's kind of where
Compressor Head came from.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.150
It's us defining the
basics of data compression

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:27.024
in these physical mediums, so.

00:05:27.024 --> 00:05:27.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:28.932
It is a beautiful show.

00:05:28.932 --> 00:05:29.640
I really like it.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:30.130
COLT MCANLIS: Oh, thank you.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:30.290
Thank you.

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:31.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
You did a great job.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.230
Google Developer Studios
did an amazing job.

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:33.780
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah, they did.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:34.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The effects
and everything is crazy.

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:35.934
COLT MCANLIS: They take
all the credit, yeah.

00:05:35.934 --> 00:05:38.980
They made me sound credible
and not like I was just

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:39.910
rambling for 30 takes.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:40.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, that's amazing.

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:42.160
COLT MCANLIS: And they cut
it together really well.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:43.030
I was impressed by that.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:43.780
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:46.506
So you now have a new series
called Performance Atlas?

00:05:46.506 --> 00:05:48.880
COLT MCANLIS: Google Cloud
Performance Atlas, yeah, yeah,

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:49.380
yeah.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.280
So the whole point of
Cloud Performance Atlas

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.700
is to sort of codify
best practices

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:57.790
and give it out to you
in bite-sized chunks

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.740
that you can take back
your engineering team,

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:03.430
or just little tidbits that you
may not have gotten access to.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:04.990
I mean, let's be
honest, building

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:06.460
a Cloud application is massive.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:07.810
There's so many moving parts.

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:10.040
It's really difficult for one
person to keep it in your head.

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:12.289
And if you're using a platform
that's also as massive,

00:06:12.289 --> 00:06:13.845
there's lots of little nuances.

00:06:13.845 --> 00:06:15.970
And sometimes performance
falls through the cracks.

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:17.590
You don't understand
how to test things.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:18.880
You don't know the
right way to profile it.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.796
If you do profile it,
you don't know if you're

00:06:20.796 --> 00:06:22.300
using the right set of things.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:23.740
You might be introducing error.

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.050
Cloud Performance Atlas
is all about helping

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.930
you walk through these steps
for the common best practices

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:30.920
and get back on
the page of things.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:34.270
MARK MANDEL: So what does
performance mean to you?

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:36.020
That seems like a very
broad topic, right?

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:36.220
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:37.180
Yeah, great question.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:40.210
So I come from a game
developer background

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.480
where I was dominantly
focused on GPU performance.

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:45.650
So how many pixels can
we get through the frame?

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:47.890
Make sure that we're staying at
60 frames a second regardless

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:49.330
of resolution, and
all that stuff.

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:51.940
So for me, it all comes down
to cycles and operations

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:54.592
that influence your bottom line,
whatever your bottom line is.

00:06:54.592 --> 00:06:56.050
So for most Cloud
applications, you

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:57.970
worry about different
things like boot time.

00:06:57.970 --> 00:06:59.650
Like if you're a web
server, you really

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:01.724
worry about how fast
your systems can boot up,

00:07:01.724 --> 00:07:03.640
how fast the content can
get from your servers

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:05.890
to the clients as
stage one, right?

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:08.500
Because if you increase
your web page load time,

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.695
you lose a billion in revenue
like Amazon said last year.

00:07:11.695 --> 00:07:13.570
On the other side of
it, once you kind of get

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:15.820
that down you need to start
doing the next step of it,

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:18.550
which is, how can
we optimize our set

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:21.640
up so that we can increase
our cost performance?

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:24.370
So getting the same
result for your end user

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:26.302
with less operations or
in a cheaper fashion.

00:07:26.302 --> 00:07:28.010
It's kind of a double
edged sword, right?

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:29.680
You want to serve things
and compute things

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.054
as fast as possible for your
users while at the same time

00:07:32.054 --> 00:07:34.240
reducing cost as much as
you can with the least

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:35.595
amount of headache for
your development team.

00:07:35.595 --> 00:07:35.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:38.110
So I was looking at
the series right now.

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:40.670
And you've published already
19 of those episodes.

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:41.920
COLT MCANLIS: So far, so good.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.003
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What are
those episodes based on?

00:07:44.003 --> 00:07:46.510
What do you cover in general?

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:49.420
Is it more like something,
I don't know, theoretical?

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:50.950
Or do you get more
into the code?

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:51.866
COLT MCANLIS: Oh yeah.

00:07:51.866 --> 00:07:54.160
So what we basically
do is, I get a chance

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:55.799
to look at some of
the common problems

00:07:55.799 --> 00:07:57.340
that developers have
on Google Cloud.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.620
And we get a chance to talk
about the real problems

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:01.953
that people are really having
in the trenches right now.

00:08:01.953 --> 00:08:04.090
And one of the things that
was my favorite episode

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:07.090
was actually profiling
App Engine boot up time.

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:08.500
Just type that
in, and the number

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:11.440
of people who still have trouble
finding out how to profile it

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:12.430
is off the chart.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:14.172
So being able to
actually sit down--

00:08:14.172 --> 00:08:16.630
what we typically do is take
an idea like that, we sit down

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:19.174
and we actually recreate
a benchmark from scratch.

00:08:19.174 --> 00:08:20.590
So we go in, we
figure out what we

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:22.131
need to test, how
we need to test it,

00:08:22.131 --> 00:08:23.560
how we need the right to test.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:26.080
Then we hand it off to
some smarter people who

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.594
make it look better, and
then remove all of the error

00:08:28.594 --> 00:08:30.010
that we may have
inserted into it.

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:31.540
We show the A and
the B, and then we

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:32.956
figure out the
right steps forward

00:08:32.956 --> 00:08:34.370
in case you find a problem.

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:36.370
So for that particular
example, one issue

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:40.150
was that if you were using
Python as an App Engine

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:44.169
standard, the particular
libraries had a larger

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:45.550
influence on boot up time.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:48.160
So if you were using
scipi or numpi,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.670
those took about 2x to 4x as
long to initialize and import

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:53.020
than other libraries did.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:54.580
And so individuals
who may be doing

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.030
some scientific computation were
getting longer boot up times

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:01.034
during server spikes and
ending up with a massive bill

00:09:01.034 --> 00:09:02.950
because their instances
went through the roof.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:05.230
And the culprit was
really that they were not

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:07.787
optimizing how they were
importing Python packages.

00:09:07.787 --> 00:09:10.120
So being able to sit down,
show people how to time that,

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.340
how to break out those
profiles, find the culprit,

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:14.048
and then optimize your
code appropriately

00:09:14.048 --> 00:09:15.670
was a really cool thing to do.

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:18.095
MARK MANDEL: So how
did you profile it?

00:09:18.095 --> 00:09:19.970
COLT MCANLIS: So for
that particular example,

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:22.300
we were able to get
those packages locally.

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:24.737
And then in Python you can
actually delay load things.

00:09:24.737 --> 00:09:25.570
So a lot of people--

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:27.070
I'm not sure if
everyone knows this,

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:28.760
but it's import on demand.

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:30.730
So once you actually
call an import statement,

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:32.230
then it's global
after that point.

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:35.890
So we can basically go through
and time particular just blank

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:37.690
functions or no-op
functions and see

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:40.660
how long they take to initialize
due to the import process.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.940
And then output all that,
create a nice little graph,

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:45.370
and can look side by side about
what's taking the extra time.

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:45.810
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:48.280
So the test was actually
basically from the outside.

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:49.720
Sending out a
request and waiting

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.580
to see how long it
takes to actually start?

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:52.120
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah, exactly.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:52.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:52.960
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah,
because that's

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:54.430
kind of the environment
we're dealing with, right?

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:55.660
I mean, a request
comes in, you get

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:56.920
a new instance that boots up.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:58.900
And we want to see
what the world looks

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:00.137
like in that environment.

00:10:00.137 --> 00:10:01.720
If we move it out
of that environment,

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:03.700
we run the risk of not
properly testing things

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:05.050
in the right way, you know?

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:07.434
Again, the opposite
of introducing error

00:10:07.434 --> 00:10:09.100
is creating a false
testing environment,

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:10.060
and that's not
what we want to do.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:10.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:12.351
So this actually sounds
really interesting.

