00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:38.548
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You were
mentioning before something

00:11:38.548 --> 00:11:41.110
that I thought was really cool
because I had no idea, which

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:43.600
is, you were talking
about Cloud storage,

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:45.760
and how apparently
there's a thing,

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:47.275
there's one of those one line--

00:11:47.275 --> 00:11:47.830
COLT MCANLIS: [LAUGHS] One line.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:49.288
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
--of documentation

00:11:49.288 --> 00:11:51.790
that you will probably miss,
which is something about,

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.220
do not upload a lot of
files at the same time

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:55.720
that have contiguous names?

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:56.960
COLT MCANLIS: Sequential
file names, yeah.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:57.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:57.880
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah, yeah.

00:11:57.880 --> 00:11:59.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: How
did you find this?

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:00.730
Did you actually read the docs?

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:01.760
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah, I
actually read the docs.

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:01.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What?

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:03.599
COLT MCANLIS: Believe
it or not, yes, yes.

00:12:03.599 --> 00:12:06.620
That's unfortunately one of the
things I do on a regular basis.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:09.960
So there's a little known
system in Google Cloud Storage

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:11.210
that a lot of people run into.

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:13.960
So if you've got a system, like
let's say a monitor camera,

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.750
or you're taking snapshots
of a time series,

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:18.670
you typically
label the file name

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:21.190
with the snapshot of the
time so you can kind of

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:23.650
look at all your time stamps,
and sort them, and figure out

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:24.190
where things are together.

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:26.023
This is very common in
scientific computing,

00:12:26.023 --> 00:12:28.262
very common in capture
based computing,

00:12:28.262 --> 00:12:29.470
and things along those lines.

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:31.810
However, what we've
seen is that customers

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:35.260
who have that scenario
often get throttled

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:38.290
when they're uploading that
content to Cloud storage.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:40.480
And they don't understand
what's going on.

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:42.220
They suspect its
a regional bucket

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:44.860
problem versus multi-regional,
the distances, maybe

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:46.030
it's parallel performance.

00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:48.000
What it actually comes down
to-- since we found this,

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:50.050
someone actually gave a
nice talk at Cloud Next

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:52.254
'17 about it, about
some of the internals,

00:12:52.254 --> 00:12:54.670
is that what happens when you
upload files to Google Cloud

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:59.710
Storage, we internally will
distribute them in parallel

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:01.510
based upon file name.

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:04.000
So what we do is, we use
that as a key to determine

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:05.030
how to separate it out.

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:06.571
And this is actually
really important

00:13:06.571 --> 00:13:09.100
because if you're uploading
two folders next to each other,

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:11.380
each folder will then have
a different file name path.

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:13.464
We know then we can push
those to different shards

00:13:13.464 --> 00:13:15.255
and handle the connectivity
for the upload.

00:13:15.255 --> 00:13:17.840
So you get nice speeds when
we use this sort of process.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:21.130
However, if you have sequential
file names in the same folder,

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:23.290
they basically all
can end up getting

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:25.930
keyed to the same upload
shard, which means that you're

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:28.670
basically bottlenecking yourself
into a serial upload process

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:29.170
there.

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:31.128
And we found this was
common enough that people

00:13:31.128 --> 00:13:32.244
were running into it.

00:13:32.244 --> 00:13:33.910
So we talked with
some of the engineers,

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.490
and we found that their
suggestion was to actually take

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:39.499
a hash of the file
name and prepend it

00:13:39.499 --> 00:13:40.790
to the actual file name itself.

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:42.290
Not the path, but the file name.

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:44.020
And then what this
does is, given

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:45.895
if you use a pretty
evenly distributed hash--

00:13:45.895 --> 00:13:47.853
I mean, it doesn't have
to be cryptographically

00:13:47.853 --> 00:13:50.110
secure or anything, but that
will allow it to actually

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:53.530
get pushed and properly load
balanced across multiple upload

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:56.170
shards for GCS so you can
actually get the maximum upload

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:57.850
speed you need from there.

00:13:57.850 --> 00:13:59.860
I mean, that's kind of
the little things, right?

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:01.840
Some stuff like that
you wouldn't expect,

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:03.610
you might miss in
the documentation.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:03.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:06.254
COLT MCANLIS: That's the whole
point of Cloud Performance

00:14:06.254 --> 00:14:10.120
Atlas, is surfacing those little
things that make big results.

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:12.550
Basically, my job is to help
