00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:48.930
As far as monitoring
and performance go,

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.250
Stackdriver has a
fantastic suite.

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:53.340
If you're running a
continuous build environment,

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.090
you should already
have monitoring set up.

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:57.700
You should have a pretty good
idea of what your average peak

00:14:57.700 --> 00:14:59.200
and alert based processes are.

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:01.120
Stackdriver can give you
all that information.

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:03.430
We just, in fact, rolled out
monitoring in Stackdriver

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:05.179
for your load balancer
so you can actually

00:15:05.179 --> 00:15:07.597
see what requests are
getting cached and whatnot.

00:15:07.597 --> 00:15:09.180
So there's a lot of
information there.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:12.177
And we have fantastic logging
inside of Google Cloud as well.

00:15:12.177 --> 00:15:14.760
So you can go through the logs,
you can see the exact numbers.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:17.343
If those things aren't helping
you point to where a problem is

00:15:17.343 --> 00:15:19.560
or an isolation or a unit
or a test case involved

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:22.360
with that, what I like to do
is start cutting away things,

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:22.860
right?

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:25.714
So if I remove
this from the path,

00:15:25.714 --> 00:15:26.880
do we get the same response?

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.365
When you start whittling
all these things down,

00:15:28.365 --> 00:15:30.990
you usually end up with a least
common factor of about two

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:32.890
or three things that
are chained together.

00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:35.265
And then from there, you can
either do micro benchmarking

00:15:35.265 --> 00:15:37.920
depending on where the issue
is, or you can go on to Twitter

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.570
