00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:02.910
able to test all these things.

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:06.069
Our next series that we're about
to launch is Cloud storage.

00:17:06.069 --> 00:17:07.770
So we're talking about all the
gotchas and all the performance

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:08.410
things there.

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:11.579
So things like sequential upload
speed, large file segmentation.

00:17:11.579 --> 00:17:14.319
Where I'm really excited to
go next is a couple of things.

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:16.079
First off is TCP BBR.

00:17:16.079 --> 00:17:18.615
I don't know if you've heard
about this or seen about this.

00:17:18.615 --> 00:17:21.329
This is one of my
favorite technologies

00:17:21.329 --> 00:17:22.859
that Google has
put on the radar.

00:17:22.859 --> 00:17:24.240
MARK MANDEL: Give us
the high level view.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:25.050
COLT MCANLIS: Wow, OK.

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:25.560
So--

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:26.440
MARK MANDEL: Super quick.

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:26.840
You can do it.

00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:27.140
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah, yeah.

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:29.730
So super quick is that
when TCP was designed,

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:32.220
it was designed with a very
specific framework in mind

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:35.262
for how the web or the
network should be used.

00:17:35.262 --> 00:17:36.720
And there were
certain difficulties

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:39.570
with callbacks and windows
and everything else.

00:17:39.570 --> 00:17:41.790
The web's different now,
and the engineers at Google

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:43.530
have found that there are
certain mathematical properties

00:17:43.530 --> 00:17:44.830
that we can take advantage of.

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:47.970
So what TCP BBR does is,
by changing and updating

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:50.370
some of the transfer protocols
in your Linux kernel,

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:53.500
you can get a 30% to 50%
performance improvement.

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:55.560
And that's not just
to other VMs that

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:59.640
have TCP BBR, that's other
systems or other APIs that

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:01.500
may just be using regular TCP.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:04.440
So this is kind of like a
free boost in performance

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:06.030
when you're doing
network transfer.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:08.910
I'm very much talking
with waving hands here

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:10.740
because there's a lot
of amazing details,

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:11.945
and a lot of brilliant
engineers who

00:18:11.945 --> 00:18:13.330
have put a lot of
brilliant time into this.

00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:14.788
So if any of you
are listening, I'm

00:18:14.788 --> 00:18:16.810
really sorry for
everything I got wrong.

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:18.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Also, come talk to us.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:20.168
COLT MCANLIS: Yes,
come talk to us, yeah.

00:18:20.168 --> 00:18:22.740
So TCP BBR, I tell you,
since the next announcement

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:25.290
for Spanner, I'm really itching
to get my hands into Spanner.

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:26.130
MARK MANDEL: Oh yeah.

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:27.210
COLT MCANLIS: I really
want to see how that

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:28.530
performs at a global level.

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:30.090
I want to find those edge cases.

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:32.100
Because as people
roll onto it, there's

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:33.869
always a teeth gnashing
process, you know?

00:18:33.869 --> 00:18:35.910
There's always, this
doesn't fit my mental model.

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:38.610
And man, I really want
to get my hands in there.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:40.210
Cloud Functions is
another great one.

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:42.459
I really want to see the
performance there, figure out

00:18:42.459 --> 00:18:43.380
how that's working.

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:45.505
And one other thing that
I'm really interested in--

00:18:45.505 --> 00:18:47.340
and I don't know how
valuable this is,

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:49.860
but I really want
to figure out if we

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:52.800
can fix a lot of the
performance problems

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:54.750
with hosting WordPress on GCP.

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:56.850
We're already amazingly
fast, but are there

00:18:56.850 --> 00:18:59.130
things that you can do
as a WordPress author

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:02.040
that GCP may give you access
to in terms of performance,

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:07.140
serving cost, DDoS resilience,
global availability?

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:09.821
From a performance level, these
are really important things,

00:19:09.821 --> 00:19:10.320
you know?

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:13.200
And hosting it on GCP, you kind
of get a lot of that for free.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:14.610
So these are some of the ideas.

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:15.990
Beginning of 2018
you'll probably

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:17.760
see some of these things
start trickling out.

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:18.885
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:19:18.885 --> 00:19:21.270
Now you mention
performance for WordPress,

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:24.860
many of our listeners, they not
only use Google Cloud Platform,

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:26.485
but they also use
also other providers.

00:19:26.485 --> 00:19:27.651
COLT MCANLIS: Oh, fantastic.

00:19:27.651 --> 00:19:30.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Do you
think that your series

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.240
helps understand performance
in general also for the Clouds?

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:35.980
Like is this something that
the skills that I'm learning,

00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:38.250
I can apply them to--
profiling, I don't know,

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:39.620
my own machines or whatever?

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:42.120
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah, I really
hope so, to be honest with you.

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:45.360
So when we do a set, like
before we talk about networking

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:47.310
and the details of
Google specific,

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:48.780
we typically have
one episode where

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:50.290
we talk about the big picture.

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:54.240
Like, how should you even
profile networking, right?

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:56.010
If you upload some
data and you time

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:59.154
it with iPerf, is that a good
number or is that a bad number?

00:19:59.154 --> 00:20:00.570
Should I be looking
at throughput?

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:01.736
Should I be looking at ping?

00:20:01.736 --> 00:20:03.627
So we typically have
a bubble up episode.

00:20:03.627 --> 00:20:05.460
Traditionally the YouTube
comments are like,

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.001
why would you do
