00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:09.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:10.050
COLT MCANLIS: And
then from there, we

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:11.425
start getting a
little bit deeper

00:20:11.425 --> 00:20:12.750
into Google level specifics.

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:14.791
But we always try to put
a nugget in there that's

00:20:14.791 --> 00:20:17.470
like, hey, at the high level,
here's how this thing works.

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:19.650
And for the videos, you'll
typically find those much

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:23.239
higher level, much more
TL;DR. And the documents,

00:20:23.239 --> 00:20:25.530
the articles that we link
with it are a little bit more

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:25.950
in detail.

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:27.870
You'll actually see source
code there, stuff you can copy

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:30.390
paste, some deeper graphs, a
little bit more explanation

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:32.950
of how some of Google's
stuff internally works.

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:36.000
So my hope for the users who
may be using other providers

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:39.060
is that they get a sense
of how things are working

00:20:39.060 --> 00:20:42.240
under the hood with
GCP, understand some

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:43.880
of those technological changes.

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:46.980
So I've been doing performance
stuff for a long time now.

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:48.492
Way too long, unfortunately.

00:20:48.492 --> 00:20:49.950
And one of the
things I always find

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:52.470
is that the crux
to performance is

00:20:52.470 --> 00:20:55.970
understanding how the
system was designed,

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:59.010
and understanding how your code
is not utilizing it that way.

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:02.154
The more you tune your
code to work exactly

00:21:02.154 --> 00:21:04.320
like the system was designed,
the better performance

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:05.153
you're going to get.

00:21:05.153 --> 00:21:07.860
So creating that mental bridge
between those two states

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:08.822
is really critical.

00:21:08.822 --> 00:21:10.780
MARK MANDEL: So now I'm
also intensely curious,

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:13.996
what's been the strangest or
the most surprising performance

00:21:13.996 --> 00:21:15.620
thing you've come
across so far in GCP?

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:16.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Hmm.

00:21:16.220 --> 00:21:17.050
COLT MCANLIS: I'm
going to cheat.

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:19.216
I'm going to kind of answer
you, but not answer you.

00:21:19.216 --> 00:21:21.389
So I won't say that
there's the weirdest thing

00:21:21.389 --> 00:21:23.430
because if you understand
how the hardware works,

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:24.680
it's just a mental disconnect.

00:21:24.680 --> 00:21:26.190
But I will say
the hardest thing.

00:21:26.190 --> 00:21:26.730
I'll tell you that one.

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:29.271
The hardest thing, this took me
probably two and a half weeks

00:21:29.271 --> 00:21:33.810
to figure out, and I had to lean
heavily on all of our Cloud DAs

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:35.680
at various capacities
to figure this out.

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:40.881
How do you profile the startup
time of Kubernetes containers?

00:21:40.881 --> 00:21:43.380
Kubernetes is known for being
really fast to boot up, right?

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:45.540
You have shared images,
you've got prefetching.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:48.570
The hosting of the image
to the instance or the pod

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:50.340
and getting that up,
that's taken away.

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:52.048
So most people forget
about the next step

00:21:52.048 --> 00:21:54.240
when you actually have
to prop your containers.

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:57.930
There is a non-trivial container
boot up time every time

00:21:57.930 --> 00:21:59.730
an instance comes up.

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:00.730
How do you time that?

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:02.280
How do you find that number?

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:04.860
If you have a bad actor
in one of your containers,

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:06.360
that can cause serious issues.

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:09.960
And in Kubernetes, you
can define for a pod

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:13.080
a set of init containers
which occur serially,

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:14.910
and then all the
other containers just

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:16.380
initialize in parallel.

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:17.840
So you can end up
in a situation.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:21.506
Let's say you're a company
that serves DRM style media.

00:22:21.506 --> 00:22:23.880
So you're an image streaming
service or a music streaming

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:24.180
service.

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:25.800
And one of your init
containers, which

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:28.380
has to occur before any of
the other things boots up,

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:31.050
is a DRM library that
properly pings and connects

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:32.850
with the services
so that you can be

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:35.160
compliant with all the systems.

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:37.476
That might have a
two second time.

00:22:37.476 --> 00:22:38.850
You might be
pinging for servers,

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:39.970
waiting for someone else.

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:41.678
But because that sits
in your init phase,

00:22:41.678 --> 00:22:44.370
it keeps the rest of
your pod from booting.

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.620
This turned out to be one
of the most difficult things

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:47.830
that I could find.

00:22:47.830 --> 00:22:49.100
Kubectl.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:49.600
Is it--?

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:50.185
MARK MANDEL: C-T-L. C-T-L.

00:22:50.185 --> 00:22:50.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cuttle.

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:50.820
MARK MANDEL: You're
right, C-T-L.

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:50.970
COLT MCANLIS: Is it
cuttle or control?

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:51.044
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cuttle.

00:22:51.044 --> 00:22:51.750
It's cuttle.

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:53.670
MARK MANDEL: C-T-L.
Well, C-T-L or control.

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:54.560
I'll go either way on that.

00:22:54.560 --> 00:22:55.510
I don't have strong feelings.

00:22:55.510 --> 00:22:56.310
COLT MCANLIS: Francesc is--

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:58.769
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The real way
is Kub-cuttle, but it's fine.

00:22:58.769 --> 00:23:00.934
COLT MCANLIS: I think this
is the new gif versus gif

00:23:00.934 --> 00:23:01.890
in the server world.

00:23:01.890 --> 00:23:02.598
Is it Kub-cuttle?

00:23:02.598 --> 00:23:03.185
Kub-control?

00:23:03.185 --> 00:23:03.490
Kub-C-T-L?

00:23:03.490 --> 00:23:04.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's gif, gif.

00:23:04.630 --> 00:23:05.210
COLT MCANLIS: Gif.

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:06.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Gif and Kub-cuttle.

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:07.528
COLT MCANLIS: I like the gif.

00:23:07.528 --> 00:23:08.430
So, this was--

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:09.513
FRANCESC CAMPOY: His face.

00:23:09.513 --> 00:23:10.049
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:10.049 --> 00:23:11.340
COLT MCANLIS: Little animosity.

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:12.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Poor Mark.

00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:13.780
COLT MCANLIS: Little
animosity in the GCP room.

00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:15.279
This was actually
the hardest thing,

00:23:15.279 --> 00:23:19.074
because Kubctl will
give you node boot time,

00:23:19.074 --> 00:23:21.240
but it won't give you
anything finer grain for that.

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:24.139
So I actually had to reach
out to Terry Ryan and Kelsey

00:23:24.139 --> 00:23:25.680
Hightower and talk
to them about some

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:27.480
of the processes they've
been through in trying

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:28.620
to boot these things up.

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:29.764
And it's all documented.

00:23:29.764 --> 00:23:31.680
If you go to GCPA, you'll
find how we actually

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:33.060
solved this, and some
techniques that you

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:34.410
can use to do it yourself.

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:38.580
But that took a lot of time to
just kind of dig in and figure

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:42.300
out what's the mentality, how
is this stuff put together?

00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:45.060
That was definitely the most
difficult, and kind of weird.

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:48.390
But nothing like we found
a ghost or an alligator

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:49.830
in the Google Cloud.

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:52.080
You know, hey, I'm not getting
the upload performance.

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:53.670
Oh yeah, there's an
alligator over there.

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:54.630
That's not-- nothing.

00:23:54.630 --> 00:23:55.151
I wish.

00:23:55.151 --> 00:23:55.650
I wish.

00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:56.500
MARK MANDEL: That
would've been interesting.

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:57.830
COLT MCANLIS: That would've
been amazing, right?

00:23:57.830 --> 00:23:58.680
In fact, you know what?

00:23:58.680 --> 00:23:59.790
That's the weirdest thing.

00:23:59.790 --> 00:24:00.070
MARK MANDEL: There's
no alligator.

00:24:00.070 --> 00:24:01.710
COLT MCANLIS: That we
didn't find an alligator.

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:02.190
MARK MANDEL: Oh, I see.

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:03.480
COLT MCANLIS: Yeah, yeah.

