00:24:54.820 --> 00:24:56.320
So, self plug, self
plug, self plug.

00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:56.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:24:56.920 --> 00:24:57.510
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:24:57.510 --> 00:24:59.176
COLT MCANLIS: Beyond
that, I really like

00:24:59.176 --> 00:25:02.340
the SRE book, the Google
Site Reliability Engineer.

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:05.660
For my background coming from
low level GPU programming,

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:08.780
the concept of a Cloud was both
familiar at a scalable level,

00:25:08.780 --> 00:25:12.740
and completely alien from a
services and ops perspective.

00:25:12.740 --> 00:25:15.350
So being able to go backwards
to forward through the SRE book

00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:17.090
was really helpful for me.

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:20.510
Likewise, I forget
the names offhand,

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:22.790
there were a couple good
Cloud engineering books

00:25:22.790 --> 00:25:25.550
that were published
in the mid '90s

00:25:25.550 --> 00:25:28.104
along with TCP/IP Illustrated
and some of those things

00:25:28.104 --> 00:25:30.020
that basically just
described the architecture

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:32.510
of the internet to help
you understand things

00:25:32.510 --> 00:25:35.360
along the lines of how caching
works, and how second rate

00:25:35.360 --> 00:25:37.707
providers work, and where
your bandwidth comes from,

00:25:37.707 --> 00:25:39.290
and latency, and all
that other stuff.

00:25:39.290 --> 00:25:40.250
